From 6d2e0d910829dc2827bc73a93b0904660376299f Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Junction 2026 team <junction2026@localhost>
Date: Sat, 14 Nov 2026 07:21:44 -0800
Subject: [PATCH 1/4] feat(training): optional early stopping on val/loss

A job can now ask to stop once val/loss has not improved for
early_stopping_patience epochs in a row (optionally by more than
early_stopping_min_delta). It is off by default.

- TrainingJobSpec and the train job payload gain both fields. Early
  stopping needs val_split > 0; a min_delta without a patience is
  dropped. Both fields are left out of the serialized spec and of the
  stored payload while early stopping is off (exclude_if), so an
  ordinary spec still validates on a trainer built before the feature,
  and a stored job still loads in a Studio without it (both models
  forbid unknown fields).
- training.early_stopping.ValLossEarlyStopping wraps Lightning's
  EarlyStopping (monitor val/loss, mode min, strict=False). It tracks the
  best epoch, which is the one ModelCheckpoint publishes, so export is
  unchanged.
- SnapFlow: early stopping watches the flow-matching phase only. val/loss
  is not comparable across the phase boundary, and stopping in phase 1
  would skip distillation. When patience runs out in phase 1, the stop
  is withdrawn, the boundary moves to the next epoch and the epoch
  budget shrinks: distillation starts from the best flow-matching
  checkpoint and still runs its full snapflow_distill_epochs.
- The run writes early_stopping.json into the model directory. It
  travels in the model artifact, so the worker reads it after a local or
  remote run and completes the job with a message such as
  "Training finished: stopped early after epoch 12 of 30; best epoch 9
  (val/loss 0.5194)". On an early stop the runner also reports that line
  at 99 % before checkpointing and export.
- The model's training summary shows the early-stopping settings.

Tests cover the spec and payload fields, the callback wiring, and real
(tiny) Lightning runs: a plateau, min_delta, a non-finite val/loss,
progress staying in range, a cancellation, and the SnapFlow hand-over
with the library's SnapFlowPhaseCallback.

